      "content": "Yes, point Q is where the velocity curve cuts the time axis for the first time, meaning the ball’s upward speed has dropped to zero – that’s the instant of maximum height.\n\n**Final Answer:** B\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $v = u + at$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $v$: final velocity (here it becomes $0\\,\\text{m·s}^{-1}$ at the top)\n  * $u$: initial upward velocity (positive value at launch)\n  * $a$: acceleration due to gravity (negative $-9.8\\,\\text{m·s}^{-2}$ because it opposes the motion)\n  * $t$: time elapsed since launch\n* **Why we use it:** The equation relates how an object's speed changes under a constant acceleration. At the highest point the speed is momentarily zero, so setting $v = 0$ lets us locate that instant on the $v\\text{–}t$ graph – the point where the line crosses the time axis.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In DBE questions, always translate the graph’s features into the underlying physics. A zero‑velocity crossing indicates a turning point (maximum or minimum). Remember to state the point (e.g., \"Q\") and the reason (\"velocity = 0, so the ball is at its greatest height\")."
