      "content": "Yes, option **C** is the correct description of the photoelectric effect.\n\n**Final Answer:** C\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\[ KE_{\\text{max}} = h f - \\phi \\]\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * \\(KE_{\\text{max}}\\): maximum kinetic energy of the ejected electron (in joules)\n  * \\(h\\): Planck's constant (≈ 6.626×10⁻³⁴ J·s)\n  * \\(f\\): frequency of the incident photon (in hertz)\n  * \\(\\phi\\): work function of the metal – the minimum energy needed to free an electron from the surface (in joules)\n* **Why we use it:** Light delivers energy in packets (photons). If a photon’s energy \\(hf\\) exceeds the metal’s work function \\(\\phi\\), the excess becomes the kinetic energy of the released electron. This equation captures that energy balance and explains why only photons above a certain frequency can cause emission, regardless of intensity.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In DBE questions, always state the process (\"electron is ejected from the metal surface\") and, if asked for a formula, write Einstein’s photoelectric equation exactly as shown. Forgetting the work function \\(\\phi\\) or mixing up emission vs. absorption costs marks."
    }
  ],
